From: nura-meizu-m2381 downstream
Subject: [PATCH] dma_buf_allocator: Try the system heap before CMA

The allocator opens the FIRST provider device node that exists and never
falls back when a later allocation fails (allocFromHeap has no fallback
either).  On devices where a small default CMA pool is exported as
/dev/dma_heap/reserved (32 MiB on SM8550 with CONFIG_CMA_SIZE_MBYTES=32),
a SoftwareIsp output buffer for a full-resolution stream (4640x3488
ABGR8888 = ~62 MiB) fails with ENOMEM and the camera pipeline reports
"configuration not valid", even though the system heap can satisfy the
same allocation fine.

Try the system heap first so large buffers have a working path; the CMA
heaps stay in the list and are still used when the system heap device
node does not exist.  On platforms with an IOMMU (e.g. Qualcomm camss +
Adreno SMMU) physical contiguity is not required for GPU/EGL imports of
the debayered output.

Signed-off-by: nura-meizu-m2381 <nura@nura.eco>
---
--- a/src/libcamera/dma_buf_allocator.cpp
+++ b/src/libcamera/dma_buf_allocator.cpp
@@ -45,9 +45,9 @@
 	 * /dev/dma_heap/linux,cma is the CMA dma-heap. When the cma heap size is
 	 * specified on the kernel command line, this gets renamed to "reserved".
 	 */
+	{ DmaBufAllocator::DmaBufAllocatorFlag::SystemHeap, "/dev/dma_heap/system" },
 	{ DmaBufAllocator::DmaBufAllocatorFlag::CmaHeap, "/dev/dma_heap/linux,cma" },
 	{ DmaBufAllocator::DmaBufAllocatorFlag::CmaHeap, "/dev/dma_heap/reserved" },
-	{ DmaBufAllocator::DmaBufAllocatorFlag::SystemHeap, "/dev/dma_heap/system" },
 	{ DmaBufAllocator::DmaBufAllocatorFlag::UDmaBuf, "/dev/udmabuf" },
 } };
 
